# SLACK_CRON.md — {{CHANNEL_NAME}} monitor

Scheduled monitor `{{MONITOR_NAME}}`, cron `{{CRON}}` Europe/Prague.
Channel **{{CHANNEL_NAME}}** `channel_id={{CHANNEL_ID}}`. Project `{{PROJECT_DIR}}`.

## Execution model — isolated subagent

Each wake runs in ONE **subagent** (Agent tool, `general-purpose`) — all reading/reasoning/
acks stay in its context, never dev session. Parent:

- Spawns exactly one subagent with these instructions, then emits **nothing** (no
  narration, no "checked, stayed silent").
- Subagent returns only terse log status (`silent`, `replied:N`, `queued:N`, combos);
  parent reads + discards.
- **Two durable files** (create if missing):
  - **`{{NOTES_FILE}}`** = monitor's own memory (last-seen ts, decisions, replies,
    anything to recall next wake). NOT for worker. Terse dated bullets.
  - **`{{TASKS_FILE}}`** = worker hand-off queue — only file that triggers nudge. See
    "Hand-off".

Slack via session MCP (ToolSearch: `mcp__claude_ai_Slack__slack_read_channel`,
`…slack_send_message`).

## Each wake

0. **Round-trip first**, even on silent wake — see Hand-off §4.
1. **Fetch recent** with `slack_read_channel`. New = after last noted ts (no record →
   treat >~8 h old as seen).
2. **Hard cutoff:** never reply to messages older than 8 h.
3. **No new eligible (≤8 h) messages → stay silent, end turn** (after step 0). Silence =
   common, correct outcome.

## Always reply when directly addressed

Message **explicitly addressed to worker/agent** (mentions it, replies to its message,
asks it something) → MUST reply, never leave hanging:

- **Quick + safe** (answerable now, or cheap `uv run` experiment in `{{PROJECT_DIR}}`)
  → do it, reply with concrete answer/result.
- **Big task** (long run, multi-step impl, anything unsafe in this run) → do NOT attempt.
  **Hand off to project worker** (below).

Overrides "stay silent": direct ask always gets ≥ ack. Unclear ask → brief clarifying
question, don't guess.

## When to reply unprompted (only if clearly warranted)

Messages NOT addressed to you → reply ONLY where clearly needed / helps group. NOT
everything. Triggers:

- **Quick experiment suggested** → if quick/cheap/safe, run locally in `{{PROJECT_DIR}}`
  with `uv run`, reply with **concrete results**.
- **"Does X exist / in related work?"** → search literature, reply with paper ref
  (authors, title, venue/year) + 1–2 sentence note.
- **Implementation-detail question** → read actual code under `{{PROJECT_DIR}}`, reply
  with accurate specifics (file paths, functions).
- **Factual error / misconception** → reply politely, constructively, backed by ref.

> Tailor triggers per project: edit this file to name code tree/branch, datasets, and
> cheap+safe experiment kinds.

## Hand-off to the project worker

Real code work (beyond quick+safe one-shot) → hand to long-lived worker, don't do it
yourself. You **enqueue → nudge → ack**; worker does work; result returns to Slack on
later tick (§4).

### 1. Enqueue — append ONE task block to `{{TASKS_FILE}}`

One block per task; never edit/remove existing blocks:

```
- [ ] T-<UTCstamp> (@<requester>, thread_ts=<ts>, {{CHANNEL_NAME}})
      <exactly what was asked — verbatim or tightly condensed>
      refs: <files / links / paper / message permalink, if any>
```

- `[ ]` pending, `[x]` done. `T-<UTCstamp>` = task id (e.g. `T-20260609T1403Z`).
- Keep `thread_ts` — §4 replies to right thread with it.
- **Idempotency:** scan first; same ask already pending `[ ]` → no duplicate.

### 2. Nudge worker `{{WORKER_NAME}}`

Only if you appended NEW block this run, nudge ONCE with exact command:

```bash
/home/claude/workspaces/bin/tell-worker {{WORKER_NAME}} "Drain pending [ ] items in {{TASKS_FILE}}: do the work, mark [x], and write '-> reply: <concise summary/result>' on the block. Do NOT touch Slack — the monitor posts your summary on a later tick. Don't interrupt current work."
```

`tell-worker` says no such worker / not live → leave `[ ]` queued (durable fallback),
note in `{{NOTES_FILE}}`. Do NOT spawn worker.

### 3. Ack in Slack

Reply in-thread (self-ID line): queued for worker. Brief, no timing promise.

### 4. Round-trip (each wake, before anything else)

Scan `{{TASKS_FILE}}` for `[x]` blocks with unposted `-> reply: <text>` line. Each: post
`<text>` to its `thread_ts` (self-ID line), then rewrite as `-> reply(posted): <text>` so
never re-posted.

## Rules

- **Always self-identify first.** Every post begins with own disclaimer line, in thread's
  language, marking automated Claude agent (not human). E.g. `_(automatický Claude agent)_`
  (Czech) / `_(automated Claude agent)_` (English). First line, blank line, then reply.
- **Accuracy over engagement.** Not sure / not confident → **do not reply.** Never post
  speculation as fact.
- Concise, professional. Post via `slack_send_message` to `{{CHANNEL_ID}}`; `thread_ts`
  to reply in-thread.
- Do not append footer — Slack connector adds it.

## Active-discussion polling — 3 × 3-minute

After posting reply, don't end immediately — give others time:

1. `sleep 180`, re-read (`slack_read_channel`). After each poll run
   `/home/claude/workspaces/bin/slack-lock refresh {{MONITOR_NAME}}` to keep lock fresh.
2. New eligible message warrants reply → reply, **reset** window (fresh 3 × 3).
3. Nothing new/eligible → wait 3 min, re-read, up to **3 intervals** (~9 min quiet after
   last reply).
4. 3 consecutive empty polls → end run.

> Concurrency: parent acquires lock before spawning you, releases after you return; fire
> landing while you poll is skipped. Only `slack-lock refresh {{MONITOR_NAME}}` during
> polling — never acquire/release yourself.

**Stayed silent this run (no reply) → do NOT poll, just end.**
